biliary obstruction but lacks specificity for common bile duct stones."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Do the combined risk factors (recent gonococcal infection treatment, chronic alcohol use, rapid weight loss) provide sufficient suspicion for Budd-Chiari syndrome despite the absence of ascites?", "summary": "Risk factors like alcohol use and weight loss raise suspicion for Budd-Chiari syndrome, but absence of ascites moderately reduces this likelihood."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of reported vaginal bleeding, amenorrhea, or adnexal tenderness on exam argue against a gynecological emergency as the primary diagnosis?", "summary": "Lack of typical gynecological symptoms moderately reduces the likelihood of a primary gynecological emergency despite β-hCG pending."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of jaundice, fever, or elevated bilirubin levels moderately reduce the likelihood of common bile duct stones as the primary diagnosis?", "summary": "Missing biliary signs like jaundice and elevated bilirubin moderately reduces suspicion for common bile duct stones."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of hepatomegaly, ascites, or coagulopathy in the history moderately reduce the likelihood of Budd-Chiari syndrome as the primary diagnosis?", "summary": "Absence of hepatomegaly, ascites, and coagulopathy moderately reduces the likelihood of Budd-Chiari syndrome despite risk factors."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.42], ["B2", 0.35], ["B5", 0.14]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.448], ["B2", 0.373], ["B2.1", 0.33]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": true, "reason": "Hemodynamic instability (80/40 mmHg, 110 bpm) with potential life-threatening causes (ectopic pregnancy, biliary obstruction, Budd-Chiari syndrome) requiring immediate stabilisation", "required_actions": ["Aggressive fluid resuscitation for hypovolemic shock", "Obtain β-hCG re