er: coverage=43%, source=both]\n\nVascular Ectasia with Hemodynamic Instability vs Ruptured Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemodynamic Instability:\n  Favours first:  abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cornea morphology, abnormal cutaneous elastic fiber morphology, abnormal facial shape, abnormal isoelectric focusing of serum transferrin\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abdominal tenderness, dizziness, fainting, hypotension\n\nVascular Ectasia with Hemodynamic Instability vs Ruptured Ovarian Cyst with Hemodynamic Instability:\n  Favours first:  abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cornea morphology, abnormal cutaneous elastic fiber morphology, abnormal facial shape, abnormal isoelectric focusing of serum transferrin\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, abdominal pain, abnormal vaginal bleeding, dizziness, feeling of fullness in the abdomen\n\nRuptured Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemodynamic Instability vs Ruptured Ovarian Cyst with Hemodynamic Instability:\n  Favours first:  abdominal tenderness, fainting, hypotension, pallor, rebound tenderness\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, abnormal vaginal bleeding, feeling of fullness in the abdomen, fever, increased urinary frequency\n\nRelated to Vascular Ectasia with Hemodynamic Instability: familial multiple nevi flammei, gastric antral vascular ectasia, lymphangiectasis\n\n[RAG context for 'A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain' (score=0.67):]\n  Source: Gynecology_Novak\n  The classic symptom triad of ectopic pregnancy is pain, amenorrhea, and vaginal bleeding. This symptom group is present in about 50% of patients and is typical in patients with a ruptured ectopic preg\n[RAG context for 'A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain' (score=0.67):]\n  Source: Ectopic Pregnancy: Diagnosis and Management. > Deterrence and Patient Education\n  Deterrence and patient education play a crucial role in reducing the risk and